{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016GK8576qw5FHYGpnFkeN5p","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"DFT binding energy atomic oxygen Cu(111) fcc site eV relative to gas-phase O atom Xu Mavrikakis\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Nanometer and sub-nanometer thin oxide films at surfaces of late transition metals\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/0409743\"},{\"title\":\"unibo.it\",\"url\":\"https://acnpsearch.tweb-dev.unibo.it/singlejournalindex/86725655\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914\"},{\"title\":\"s11433 012 4868 5\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11433-012-4868-5.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Structural and electronic properties of atomic oxygen adsorption on Cu(111) surface: A first-principles investigation\",\"url\":\"https://bvbvf1.bib-bvb.de/vufind/PrimoRecord/cdi_chongqing_primary_44769476\"},{\"title\":\"scielo.br\",\"url\":\"https://scielo.br/j/mr/a/7JTVH5pDWCwb78HgZ8XXKRG/?lang=en\"},{\"title\":\"Adsorption and dissociation of O2 on Cu(111):: thermochemistry, reaction barrier and the effect of strain\",\"url\":\"https://data.zhenzhi.com/details/1/867115130\"},{\"title\":\"PhysRevB 73 165424\",\"url\":\"https://condensed.physics.usyd.edu.au/old_website/papers_aloy/PhysRevB_73_165424.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"August 19, 2004\",\"url\":\"https://www.pnnl.gov/publications/effect-sn-reactivity-cu-surfaces\"},{\"title\":\"The adsorption and dissociation of O2 molecular precursors on Cu:: the effect of steps\",\"url\":\"https://data.zhenzhi.com/details/1/888079892\"}]\n\nThe search turned up the Xu and Mavrikakis result you were after, along with a few related DFT values that differ from it.\n\n- **Xu & Mavrikakis (Surf. Sci. 2001):** The paper's abstract reports the atomic oxygen binding energy on Cu(111) as about -4.3 eV relative to a gas-phase O atom, with threefold hollow sites preferred Atomic oxygen is found to prefer threefold hollows. with a binding energy of ca. -4.3 eV with respect to a gas-phase O atom. The same abstract notes that expansive surface strain strengthens O binding Expansive strain parallel to the surface plane is shown to enhance the binding of atomic and molecular oxygen on the surface. I only saw the abstract, so I could not confirm the fcc-versus-hcp split from the paper itself.\n- **Other DFT values:** A later Cu(211) study gives about -4.3 eV for O on Cu(111) as well, and about -4.5 eV on the stepped Cu(211) surface the binding energy of atomic oxygen is -4.5 eV at its preferred site on the relaxed Cu(211) surface vs. -4.3 eV at its preferred site on the relaxed Cu(111) surface. An arXiv study reports -5.000 eV for fcc and -4.905 eV for hcp, using a different convention and setup, with threefold hollow sites, both fcc and hcp, ... are the energetically preferred binding sites for O* with a binding energy of -5 eV and -4.905 eV, respectively. The gap from -4.3 eV is a sign of how sensitive these numbers are to the functional, slab model, and reference, so the Xu–Mavrikakis value should be quoted with its own conditions.\n- **Reference convention:** One Cu(111) paper defines binding energy as a positive number indicates that the adsorption is exothermic \"stable\" with respect to the free oxygen atom, which is the opposite sign from the negative values above. Check the sign convention before comparing numbers across papers.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9e56a60c-1c33-400a-a0e7-825fccd9106a","uuid":"e4f696a1-aaec-445e-996b-2db44954871f","timestamp":"2026-10-31T21:54:32.123Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"DFT binding energy atomic oxygen Cu(111) fcc site eV relative to gas-phase O atom Xu Mavrikakis","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01JzV5Krj2GNGWKNupDszEfX","content":[{"title":"Nanometer and sub-nanometer thin oxide films at surfaces of late transition metals","u