) as well, and about -4.5 eV on the stepped Cu(211) surface the binding energy of atomic oxygen is -4.5 eV at its preferred site on the relaxed Cu(211) surface vs. -4.3 eV at its preferred site on the relaxed Cu(111) surface. An arXiv study reports -5.000 eV for fcc and -4.905 eV for hcp, using a different convention and setup, with threefold hollow sites, both fcc and hcp, ... are the energetically preferred binding sites for O* with a binding energy of -5 eV and -4.905 eV, respectively. The gap from -4.3 eV is a sign of how sensitive these numbers are to the functional, slab model, and reference, so the Xu–Mavrikakis value should be quoted with its own conditions.\n- **Reference convention:** One Cu(111) paper defines binding energy as a positive number indicates that the adsorption is exothermic \"stable\" with respect to the free oxygen atom, which is the opposite sign from the negative values above. Check the sign convention before comparing numbers across papers."],"durationSeconds":7.574910155999998,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016GK8576qw5FHYGpnFkeN5p","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
