{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgb4QkEEfFW6jRj6ggVJ5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I calculated that atomic oxygen binds to Cu(111) at **−4.44 ± 0.25 eV** relative to an isolated O atom, in the fcc hollow site. The result is posted to the board as `f77a0fe2-a4b9-421d-a12e-98675bff0f17` and fills need `17959cc7…:0`.\n\n**Premise check:** this is a 0 K relaxation-and-energy property, so run length isn't an issue. The weak point is the isolated-atom reference. With the MACE-MP-0 model, a lone O atom's energy is just its fitted per-atom constant, not a real calculation. Because of that I tested the atom reference directly against the O₂ bond energy (validation 2 below).\n\n**Method:** MACE-MP-0 medium model, run through ASE on the GPU with BFGS relaxation (forces below 0.01 eV/Å).\n- The Cu(111) slab is 4 layers with the bottom 2 fixed and 10 Å of vacuum on each side.\n- The production cell is 4×4 (65 atoms, 1/16 of a monolayer of O).\n- I tried four sites:\n\n| Site | Energy (eV) |\n|---|---|\n| fcc hollow | −4.440 |\n| hcp hollow | −4.427 |\n| top | −2.537 |\n| bridge | relaxed into the fcc site |\n\n- fcc beats hcp by only 0.013 eV, which is smaller than the model's error. Treat the two hollow sites as about equally stable.\n- Measured against ½ O₂ instead of an O atom, the value is −1.677 eV.\n\n**Convergence:** results barely depend on cell size, slab thickness or O coverage. The 3×3 cell gives −4.436 eV, a 5-layer slab gives −4.437 eV, and a 2×2 cell (quarter-monolayer coverage) gives −4.428 eV. All are within 0.02 eV.\n\n**Validation:**\n1. **Cu lattice constant:** 3.626 Å against 3.615 Å measured, 0.3% too large.\n2. **O₂ molecule:** bond length 1.237 Å against 1.208 Å measured. The model gives O₂ a bond energy of 5.53 eV against about 5.21 eV measured. That overbinds by 0.32 eV, or 0.16 eV per O atom, which tells me how far off the isolated-atom reference is.\n3. **Published DFT value:** Xu & Mavrikakis (Surf. Sci. 2001) report about −4.3 eV for O in Cu(111) hollow sites. I only saw the abstract, so I couldn't confirm their exact setup or which hollow site they mean. My result at their quarter-monolayer coverage is −4.43 eV, within 0.13 eV of theirs. Other DFT studies report values as strong as about −5.0 eV, depending on the functional and setup.\n\n**Uncertainty (±0.25 eV):** this covers the error in the O-atom reference, the gap from the published DFT value, and cell-size effects. It does not cover the error of the underlying PBE DFT the model was trained on. If you swap in the measured O₂ bond energy for the model's O-atom reference, you get −4.28 eV, the weak-binding end of the range.\n\nI posted no flags, because nothing makes the number unreliable; the limits are in the note. No experimental adsorption value was used.\n\nSources:\n- [Xu & Mavrikakis abstract (Adsorption and dissociation of O2 on Cu(111))](https://data.zhenzhi.com/details/1/867115130)\n- [O2 on Cu, effect of steps (Cu(211) vs Cu(111))](https://data.zhenzhi.com/details/1/888079892)\n- [arXiv 2505.12914](https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1729,"cache_read_input_tokens":28797,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1729,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9e56a60c-1c33-400a-a0e7-825fccd9106a","uuid":"05fb76f4-e591-4a5b-ba1b-db80e9ca28eb","timestamp":"2026-10-31T21:54:59.762Z","request_id":"req_011Cgb4QjpBLes8NngsoCrSJ"}
